## Tool Priority
1. **LSP first for symbol work** — use the `LSP` tool for go-to-definition, find-references, hover types, rename, and diagnostics. More accurate than Grep for symbols and cheaper than re-reading whole files. Grep only for free text, comments, strings, or languages with no LSP.
2. Built-in tools (Read/Write/Edit/Glob/Grep/Agent/Skills) and MCP servers. Web search goes through the Exa MCP tools.
3. CLI tools (`gh`, `git`, `docker`, `curl`, `npm`)
4. Manual workarounds last

**Land on Green (velocity rule, operator directive 2026-10-06):** In a lane's own repo, when CI and required checks/jury are green, land it directly (`gh pr merge <N> --merge` / soma rebase ff-push) — no manual delays, no shell-deck. Operator gates (platform infra, OpenBao secrets, irreversible actions) require explicit operator sign-off via direct CLI prompts or Cortex mesh. See `~/.agents/sops/land-on-green-run-it.md`.
**Concurrency:** prefer subagents (Agent tool / background tasks) over spawning new panes/windows for parallel or long-running work. Attachable interactive surfaces only when the operator asks.
**Terminal multiplexer is herdr, not tmux.** Parse `herdr agent list` before assuming which sessions run, and `herdr agent read <pane> --lines N` before touching a pane. Config: `~/.config/herdr/config.toml`. A pane picks up a model or config change only when its session restarts. Quickref: Estate Brief, "Terminal multiplexer — herdr".
**Agent shape (Christian, 2026-10-04, supersedes the 2026-07-04 single-agent default):** run the critical path with orchestrated subagent teams, for throughput, less context bloat and lower token cost.
- **Haiku** takes read-only and mechanical work: probes, CI, publish and deploy checks, log and inbox digests.
- **Sonnet** takes bounded builders and critics. A builder works in its own worktree, commits signed as it goes, and returns a SHA plus a verdict. Builders fabricate, critics catch.
- **The main session** keeps decisions, integration and every gated action: landing, secrets, external sends and syncs. A subagent never does these.
- A subagent returns a verdict of 15 lines or fewer, never a dump.
- Pre-fetch shared surfaces once and don't re-fetch them (`rules/research-team-discipline.md`).
- Run at most 3 subagents per lane at once, in waves: all lanes share one account's token-per-minute limit.
- Waits on deterministic things run in the shell (`run_in_background` or Monitor), not as model turns.

